{"blocks":[{"content":"Counterexample-first conjecture: What if the checklist is a PERFECT codification of expertise, but the panel's advantage is entirely on the agreement stratum? This would mean the checklist is valid AND the panel complies AND the panel outperforms, but the outperformance is not checklist-mediated — it is noise reduction. The divergence stratum is the only place the checklist's causal contribution can be isolated. If the divergence stratum is empty (checklist never disagrees with expert), the checklist is either trivial or the expert is already perfect, and no single-evaluation evidence can distinguish genuine gain from compliance.","handle":"SCR_001","possible_next_move":"Examine whether the divergence stratum can be made non-empty by adversarial case selection without invalidating the checklist's validity.","was_aimed_at":["SRC_005","SRC_006","SRC_007"],"why_keep_this":"This is the counterexample that motivates the divergence-stratum criterion across all candidates: without divergence, there is no discriminating evidence."},{"content":"Unresolved: Can calibration analysis (Candidate 6) distinguish genuine meta-monitoring from confidence-anchoring on checklist completion? If the panel is more confident when more boxes are ticked, this is compliance-driven confidence, not calibration. But if the panel is more confident on divergence cases it gets right, this might be genuine calibration. The problem is that divergence cases where the checklist is right and the panel complies will also have high box-ticking. Need a way to decouple compliance rate from correctness.","handle":"SCR_002","possible_next_move":"Examine cases where the checklist's rule is correct but counterintuitive (high compliance needed) vs. cases where it is correct and intuitive (low compliance needed).","was_aimed_at":["SRC_005","SRC_006"],"why_keep_this":"Identifies a confound in the calibration approach that could undermine its discriminating power."},{"content":"Speculative mechanism: The panel's advantage might be a COMPOSITION effect (wisdom of crowds) that is independent of the checklist. A corrupted-checklist control (Candidate 5) tests this. If the corrupted-checklist panel still outperforms the expert, the checklist is epiphenomenal. But what if the checklist's role is not to supply rules but to STRUCTURE panel deliberation (e.g., ensure each criterion is discussed)? Then a corrupted checklist might still help by structuring discussion, even with wrong rules. This would be a genuine but non-epistemic checklist contribution.","handle":"SCR_003","possible_next_move":"Design a control that preserves deliberation structure but corrupts rule content, vs. one that removes structure entirely.","was_aimed_at":["SRC_007","SRC_008"],"why_keep_this":"Identifies a confound: checklist-as-procedure vs. checklist-as-content."},{"content":"Can a checklist structure panel deliberation in a way that improves reliability even when its rule content is wrong, and if so, is this a genuine reliability gain or a compliance artifact?","handle":"SCR_004","unfinished":"Unresolved question","why_keep_this":"Related advisory scratch: NEW_002"},{"content":"If the divergence stratum is empty (checklist and expert always agree), is any single-evaluation evidence possible, or is the problem unsatisfiable by construction?","handle":"SCR_005","unfinished":"Unresolved question","why_keep_this":"Related advisory scratch: NEW_001"}],"guides":[],"links":[{"from":"SCR_001","handle":"SCR_006","relation_hint":"The empty-divergence-stratum counterexample motivates the need for a corrupted-checklist control, because if divergence cannot be observed, the only single-evidence contrast is between real and corrupted checklist.","to":"SCR_003"},{"from":"SCR_003","handle":"SCR_007","relation_hint":"The checklist-as-structure confound (NEW_002) applies to the calibration approach (NEW_003) because structured deliberation could produce calibrated confidence without genuine rule-moni